1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,192
By the end of this guide you will know what the double push is, and how to learn it step by step.

2
00:00:06,192 --> 00:00:11,363
You will also know where it belongs on the track and what it asks of your skates.

3
00:00:11,363 --> 00:00:17,682
Ask experienced inline speed skaters when they finally got fast, and many will point to one moment.

4
00:00:17,682 --> 00:00:20,172
It was the day the double push clicked.

5
00:00:20,172 --> 00:00:27,769
It is the stroke that lets marathon and track skaters hold high speeds for long stretches while looking almost relaxed.

6
00:00:27,769 --> 00:00:31,599
It is also one of the most misunderstood moves in the sport.

7
00:00:31,599 --> 00:00:35,748
From the outside it just looks like normal skating done smoothly.

8
00:00:35,748 --> 00:00:37,344
It is a feel first skill.

9
00:00:37,344 --> 00:00:42,387
So the aim here is a clear picture in your head and a sensible way to build it.

10
00:00:43,088 --> 00:00:44,885
Start with a classic stroke.

11
00:00:44,885 --> 00:00:49,893
You set the skate down under you, roll onto an edge, and push out to the side.

12
00:00:49,893 --> 00:00:53,553
Then you bring the leg back and repeat on the other side.

13
00:00:53,553 --> 00:00:55,158
That is one push per leg.

14
00:00:55,158 --> 00:00:59,460
The double push adds a second push at the start of the same stroke.

15
00:00:59,460 --> 00:01:06,651
Instead of setting the skate straight down, you place it slightly to the inside of your body, on an inside edge.

16
00:01:06,651 --> 00:01:08,192
You push inward briefly.

17
00:01:08,192 --> 00:01:13,906
Then you roll the skate across to the outside edge and deliver the familiar outward push.

18
00:01:13,906 --> 00:01:15,961
Two pushes, one leg, one stroke.

19
00:01:16,661 --> 00:01:19,239
Here is the difference seen from above.

20
00:01:19,239 --> 00:01:23,667
The single push travels one way, straight out from the centre line.

21
00:01:23,667 --> 00:01:29,748
The double push first curves inward on an inside edge, then rolls across and pushes outward.

22
00:01:29,748 --> 00:01:34,903
In a single push stroke, the moment you set the skate down gives you no drive.

23
00:01:34,903 --> 00:01:36,292
It is just a landing.

24
00:01:36,292 --> 00:01:40,323
In a double push, that landing becomes an active inward push.

25
00:01:40,323 --> 00:01:43,893
So you make force across a longer part of each stroke.

26
00:01:43,893 --> 00:01:46,338
More of your stride does useful work.

27
00:01:46,338 --> 00:01:53,609
That is why skaters can hold a higher cruising speed without a higher heart rate, once the move feels natural.

28
00:01:54,309 --> 00:01:57,778
The heart of the double push is the inside edge push.

29
00:01:57,778 --> 00:02:05,370
When you set the skate down slightly inside your body line and push inward, you load the inside edge of your wheels.

30
00:02:05,370 --> 00:02:08,250
You use it to send force toward your centre.

31
00:02:08,250 --> 00:02:14,992
This feels strange at first, because most skaters are taught to keep the set down right under the body.

32
00:02:14,992 --> 00:02:18,723
Learning to trust that inside edge is the biggest hurdle.

33
00:02:18,723 --> 00:02:21,406
It is why the move takes weeks, not days.

34
00:02:21,406 --> 00:02:24,483
Once the inside push is spent, relax the ankle.

35
00:02:24,483 --> 00:02:29,522
Let the skate roll from the inside edge, through flat, onto the outside edge.

36
00:02:29,522 --> 00:02:31,682
That roll is smooth, not a flick.

37
00:02:31,682 --> 00:02:33,973
Then deliver the main outward push.

38
00:02:33,973 --> 00:02:37,377
The two pushes should feel like one connected sweep.

39
00:02:38,077 --> 00:02:41,944
This front view shows what the wheel does across one stroke.

40
00:02:41,944 --> 00:02:45,296
It starts on the inside edge, where you push inward.

41
00:02:45,296 --> 00:02:47,165
It rolls across through flat.

42
00:02:47,165 --> 00:02:50,840
Then it reaches the outside edge, where you push outward.

43
00:02:50,840 --> 00:02:53,289
The inward push loads the inside edge.

44
00:02:53,289 --> 00:02:55,867
The outward push loads the outside edge.

45
00:02:55,867 --> 00:02:59,606
Notice that the roll in the middle is not a separate move.

46
00:02:59,606 --> 00:03:02,958
It is the bridge that joins the two pushes together.

47
00:03:02,958 --> 00:03:06,825
If you rush it, the stroke breaks into two separate efforts.

48
00:03:06,825 --> 00:03:09,210
If you let the ankle relax, it flows.

49
00:03:09,910 --> 00:03:12,484
The double push is a straightaway stroke.

50
00:03:12,484 --> 00:03:17,820
In a corner you are crossing over, and the crossover already keeps both legs working.

51
00:03:17,820 --> 00:03:21,649
Trying to double push through a bend just breaks your rhythm.

52
00:03:21,649 --> 00:03:23,470
The skill is to link the two.

53
00:03:23,470 --> 00:03:31,756
Finish your last straight stroke cleanly, drop into the crossover, and come out of the corner straight into a connected double push.

54
00:03:31,756 --> 00:03:34,455
That exit is where most skaters lose speed.

55
00:03:34,455 --> 00:03:39,289
The first stroke out of the corner is rushed and never loads the inside edge.

56
00:03:39,289 --> 00:03:45,064
On a 200 metre track the straights are short, so you may fit only two or three full strokes.

57
00:03:45,064 --> 00:03:49,584
On the road and in a marathon, the straights run for hundreds of metres.

58
00:03:49,584 --> 00:03:52,974
There, the double push becomes the stroke you live in.

59
00:03:52,974 --> 00:03:59,314
In a pack, it keeps your feet closer to your centre line, so you can sit close behind another skater.

60
00:04:00,014 --> 00:04:04,578
The double push loads both edges of the wheel hard, again and again.

61
00:04:04,578 --> 00:04:06,792
So your equipment has to keep up.

62
00:04:06,792 --> 00:04:12,698
A flexy or badly mounted frame robs you of the crisp edge changes the stroke depends on.

63
00:04:12,698 --> 00:04:19,946
A stiff race frame sends your inside and outside pushes straight into the wheels instead of soaking them up.

64
00:04:19,946 --> 00:04:21,758
Wheels matter just as much.

65
00:04:21,758 --> 00:04:26,188
A wheel that is too hard for you will slip during the inside push.

66
00:04:26,188 --> 00:04:31,288
Match firmness to your body weight and the smoothness of your usual surface.

67
00:04:31,288 --> 00:04:36,187
Finally, bearings decide how much of your speed survives between strokes.

68
00:04:36,187 --> 00:04:41,153
Keep them clean, and give them a single drop of bearing oil after a clean.

69
00:04:41,153 --> 00:04:47,126
A dry or gritty bearing scrubs off exactly the glide the double push is built to protect.

70
00:04:47,826 --> 00:04:51,368
Do not try a full double push at speed on day one.

71
00:04:51,368 --> 00:04:52,713
Build it in layers.

72
00:04:52,713 --> 00:04:53,422
Stage one.

73
00:04:53,422 --> 00:04:59,584
Skate slowly and set each skate clearly inside your body line, then push out as normal.

74
00:04:59,584 --> 00:05:05,037
This teaches your body to accept a set down that is not right underneath you.

75
00:05:05,037 --> 00:05:07,162
Spend a whole session on that.

76
00:05:07,162 --> 00:05:07,871
Stage two.

77
00:05:07,871 --> 00:05:15,520
At a gentle roll, set the skate inside, press onto the inside edge, and feel it push you toward your centre.

78
00:05:15,520 --> 00:05:17,645
Then roll across and push out.

79
00:05:17,645 --> 00:05:20,336
It will feel clumsy, and that is fine.

80
00:05:20,336 --> 00:05:21,186
Stage three.

81
00:05:21,186 --> 00:05:28,411
Only when the inward push and the roll feel connected, add tempo and blend it into full speed skating.

82
00:05:28,411 --> 00:05:30,465
Slow and correct comes first.

83
00:05:30,465 --> 00:05:31,669
Speed comes last.

84
00:05:32,369 --> 00:05:34,920
Here is a useful check between stages.

85
00:05:34,920 --> 00:05:37,002
It is the one foot glide drill.

86
00:05:37,002 --> 00:05:39,285
Roll on one skate at walking pace.

87
00:05:39,285 --> 00:05:43,112
Tip it from the inside edge to the outside edge and back.

88
00:05:43,112 --> 00:05:46,604
Feel the wheel change direction under you each time.

89
00:05:46,604 --> 00:05:54,595
If you cannot steer a single skate with its edges while gliding, you are not ready to load those edges inside a stroke.

90
00:05:54,595 --> 00:05:58,221
Do five minutes of this at the start of every session.

91
00:05:58,221 --> 00:06:01,914
It builds the ankle control the double push depends on.

92
00:06:01,914 --> 00:06:08,293
Rushing straight to race pace usually bakes in a rushed, broken stroke that is hard to unlearn.

93
00:06:08,993 --> 00:06:11,275
The most common error is faking it.

94
00:06:11,275 --> 00:06:18,054
Many skaters wiggle their hips and believe they are double pushing, but they never load the inside edge.

95
00:06:18,054 --> 00:06:23,204
If you do not feel a clear inward push against that edge, you are just swaying.

96
00:06:23,204 --> 00:06:25,616
Slow down and hunt for that pressure.

97
00:06:25,616 --> 00:06:29,528
The second mistake is a jerky change between the two pushes.

98
00:06:29,528 --> 00:06:33,505
The roll must come from a relaxing ankle, not a sudden stamp.

99
00:06:33,505 --> 00:06:38,850
The third is setting the skate too far inside, so you cannot recover your balance.

100
00:06:38,850 --> 00:06:42,631
Keep the inside set down modest until your edges catch up.

101
00:06:42,631 --> 00:06:44,913
The fourth hides in the upper body.

102
00:06:44,913 --> 00:06:46,803
Keep the torso quiet and low.

103
00:06:46,803 --> 00:06:50,845
Swing the arms straight forward and back, not across the body.

104
00:06:50,845 --> 00:06:58,342
Filmed from behind, a good double push shows the skates weaving while the head and shoulders track almost straight.

105
00:06:59,042 --> 00:07:02,982
Do you need the double push to enjoy inline speed skating?

106
00:07:02,982 --> 00:07:03,186
No.

107
00:07:03,186 --> 00:07:06,854
Plenty of skaters stay quick with a clean single push.

108
00:07:06,854 --> 00:07:11,745
Learn that first, then add the double push when your edges are reliable.

109
00:07:11,745 --> 00:07:13,239
How long does it take?

110
00:07:13,239 --> 00:07:22,206
Most skaters need several weeks of patient, slow practice before it feels connected, and months before it is automatic at race pace.

111
00:07:22,206 --> 00:07:25,806
Progress comes in sudden jumps, so do not lose heart.

112
00:07:25,806 --> 00:07:29,406
Does it work on bigger wheels and three wheel frames?

113
00:07:29,406 --> 00:07:29,678
Yes.

114
00:07:29,678 --> 00:07:35,112
Taller wheels roll further per stroke and reward a smooth double push even more.

115
00:07:35,112 --> 00:07:39,256
But a taller setup tips onto its edges a little less quickly.

116
00:07:39,256 --> 00:07:43,400
So give yourself extra slow sessions to find the inside edge.

117
00:07:44,100 --> 00:07:46,191
The double push is not a trick.

118
00:07:46,191 --> 00:07:49,362
It is a more complete way of using each stroke.

119
00:07:49,362 --> 00:07:59,618
Set the skate slightly inside your body, push against the inside edge, roll smoothly across to the outside edge, and finish with the usual outward push.

120
00:07:59,618 --> 00:08:03,802
You turn the dead landing of ordinary skating into real drive.

121
00:08:03,802 --> 00:08:09,672
Learn it slowly, keep it for the straights, and add tempo only once it feels connected.

122
00:08:09,672 --> 00:08:16,014
The full guide is on the LiveLica blog, with every diagram and the frequently asked questions.

123
00:08:16,014 --> 00:08:18,106
The link is in the description.
